From a9c729b0cdf5fb32dae4c0dc4195f7d4fbe64d3d Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WWE13 Recomp <wwe13-recomp@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 25 Nov 2026 11:19:18 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 121/124] threading: blocking multi-object waits;
 user-interactive QoS on Apple

PosixConditionBase::WaitMultiple polled: try-lock every handle, check, unlock, and sleep a
remaining time cast down to whole milliseconds - zero inside every 1 ms alertable slice - so a
thread parked in a multi-object wait spun. On an iPad the game's audio streaming thread and
the audio worker each burned most of a core that way (80-94 % and 65-73 %); on a Mac in menus
the whole game used 221 % CPU before and 53 % after, reaching the same point.

Waiters now register with every handle; a handle that changes state (NotifyAll, called under its
mutex wherever cond_.notify_all() was) bumps the waiter's generation and wakes it. Wait-all
locks the distinct handles in address order. Unit tests cover wake-up, lowest-index WaitAny,
WaitAll with a semaphore, timeouts without spinning, and 40,000 concurrent releases.

macOS / iOS schedule by QoS class, so game threads are created user-interactive (the class a
foreground app's own threads use); WWE13_THREAD_QOS=off or =initiated changes that.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
